#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:131
msgid "Differences in User Interfaces"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:133
msgid "No Indexing"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:135
msgid ""
"If you've used WebStorm on large projects, you know the wait. Opening a "
"project with many dependencies can mean watching \"Indexing...\" for "
"anywhere from 30 seconds to several minutes. WebStorm indexes your entire "
"codebase and `node_modules` to power its code intelligence, and re-indexes "
"when dependencies change."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:137
msgid ""
"Zed doesn't index. You open a folder and start coding immediately—no "
"progress bars, no \"Indexing paused\" banners. File search and navigation "
"stay fast regardless of project size or how many `node_modules` dependencies "
"you have."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:139
msgid ""
"WebStorm's index enables features like finding all usages across your entire "
"codebase, tracking import hierarchies, and flagging unused exports project-"
"wide. Zed relies on language servers for this analysis, which may not cover "
"as much ground."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:141 src/migrate/webstorm.md:167
#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:186 src/migrate/webstorm.md:221
msgid "**How to adapt:**"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:143
msgid ""
"Search symbols across the project with `Cmd+O` (powered by the TypeScript "
"language server)"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:144
msgid "Find files by name with `Cmd+Shift+O`"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:145
msgid "Use `Cmd+Shift+F` for text search—it stays fast even in large monorepos"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:146
msgid ""
"Run `tsc --noEmit` or `eslint .` from the terminal when you need deeper "
"project-wide analysis"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:148
msgid "LSP vs. Native Language Intelligence"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:150
msgid ""
"WebStorm has its own JavaScript and TypeScript analysis engine built by "
"JetBrains. This engine understands your code deeply: it resolves types, "
"tracks data flow, knows about framework-specific patterns, and offers "
"specialized refactorings."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:152
msgid ""
"Zed uses the Language Server Protocol (LSP) for code intelligence. For "
"JavaScript and TypeScript, Zed supports:"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:154
msgid ""
"**vtsls** (default) — Fast TypeScript language server with excellent "
"performance"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:155
msgid ""
"**typescript-language-server** — The standard TypeScript LSP implementation"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:156
msgid "**ESLint** — Linting integration"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:157
msgid "**Prettier** — Code formatting (built-in)"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:159
msgid ""
"The TypeScript LSP experience is well-supported. You get accurate "
"completions, type checking, go-to-definition, and find-references. The "
"experience is comparable to VS Code, which uses the same underlying "
"TypeScript services."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:161
msgid "Where you might notice differences:"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:163
msgid ""
"Framework-specific intelligence (Angular templates, Vue SFCs) may be less "
"integrated"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:164
msgid ""
"Some complex refactorings (extract component with proper imports) may be "
"less sophisticated"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:165
msgid ""
"Auto-import suggestions depend on what the language server knows about your "
"project"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:169
msgid ""
"Use `Alt+Enter` for available code actions—the list will vary by language "
"server"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:170
msgid ""
"Ensure your `tsconfig.json` is properly configured so the language server "
"understands your project structure"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:171
msgid ""
"Use Prettier for consistent formatting (it's enabled by default for JS/TS)"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:172
msgid ""
"For code inspection similar to WebStorm's \"Inspect Code,\" check the "
"Diagnostics panel (`Cmd+6`)—ESLint and TypeScript together catch many of the "
"same issues"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:174
msgid "No Project Model"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:176
msgid ""
"WebStorm manages projects through `.idea` folders containing XML "
"configuration files, framework detection, and run configurations. This model "
"lets WebStorm remember your project settings, manage npm scripts through the "
"UI, and persist run/debug setups."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:178
msgid ""
"Zed takes a different approach: a project is just a folder. There's no setup "
"wizard, no framework detection dialog, no project structure to configure."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:180
msgid "What this means in practice:"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:182
msgid ""
"Run configurations aren't a thing. Define reusable commands in `tasks.json` "
"instead. Note that your existing `.idea/` configurations won't carry over—"
"you'll set up the ones you need fresh."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:183
msgid ""
"npm scripts live in the terminal. Run `npm run dev`, `pnpm build`, or `yarn "
"test` directly—there's no dedicated npm panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:184
msgid ""
"No framework detection. Zed treats React, Angular, Vue, and vanilla JS/TS "
"the same way."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:188
msgid ""
"Create a `.zed/settings.json` in your project root for project-specific "
"settings"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/webstorm.md:189
msgid ""
"Define common commands in `tasks.json` (open via Command Palette: `zed: open "
"tasks`):"
msgstr ""

